@@ -0,0 +1,123 @@
+# AMD Anti-Lag 2 and XeSS 30 → 120 latency work
+
+Status: **source implementation with CPU contract tests; no rebuilt Windows player.**
+
+The user runs XeSS 4X from a 30 FPS base and wants to reduce frame-generation
+delay. The original AMD portable ZIP was downloaded from the official v2.1.9
+release. Its SHA-256 matches the publisher's SHA256SUMS.txt:
+`f7113db09eff74065cfd9b7044df8db3c301ac5b7ff80f692da56fee2c2bf377`.
+The working source is tag v2.1.9, peeled commit
+`d442b4e5ac5907fffd4c20e9e876a324708750f2`.
+
+## XeSS-specific work
+
+XeSS already uses XeLL, which Intel requires with frame generation. The native
+AMD latency path is deliberately disabled for XeSS. We cannot correctly label
+the provider's hidden generated presents by tagging only Veyra's real presents,
+and a second source wait could conflict with XeLL. We preserve XeLL, its IDs and
+markers, the existing Intel scheduler, the tail-frame limiter and the existing
+2X path.
+
+An opt-in **XeSS lower-latency fallback** preference appears in the list and node
+editors when XeSS is selected. It applies only to live capture and only when the
+existing MFG pacing hook reaches its wall-clock fallback. It does not replace
+the primary Intel scheduler. When the normal scheduler handles a frame, this
+preference has no timing effect.
+
+In that fallback, the first generated frame occupies the current slot instead
+of waiting another output interval. Subsequent deadlines use the same absolute
+burst grid. The period comes from consecutive source PTS, when available, so
+the application's own waits are not learned as a slower input source. When a
+slot is already late it adds no catch-up delay. Unknown source timing retains
+the existing measured-period fallback. Native output frames are not discarded.
+
+For the isolated 30 FPS / 4X timing contract, generated deadlines change from
+approximately +8.33/+16.67/+25 ms to +0/+8.33/+16.67 ms when all three are paced
+by that fallback. If the provider owns the tail, its existing tail behavior
+still takes precedence. These are scheduled CPU deadlines, **not measured
+end-to-end latency improvements**. Earlier intermediate frames can make spacing
+less even relative to the provider-owned real/tail frames. The option defaults
+off and needs a hardware A/B test before recommending it.
+
+No change to A/B history, optical flow, image quality, export, source PTS,
+provider binaries on disk, or output FPS claims is part of this patch.
+
+## Native AMD Anti-Lag 2
+
+Added the official MIT-licensed DirectX 12 SDK header at fixed commit
+`390aa4a8c8655d0ae6e90079db2c85e103a96da3`, its license and provenance. The
+application initializes one context on the selected AMD D3D12 device, updates
+once before selecting the next accepted source, marks submitted source work,
+and identifies real/generated native presents separately. Waiting polls reuse
+the pending input. Driver errors or four excessive waits within sixteen input
+updates stop the session. Contexts are disabled/released at teardown and graph
+replacement. Reconfiguration permits an explicit retry.
+
+This opt-in path is limited to live capture with native Veyra-owned
+presentation. Radeon selection is UI eligibility; actual support is determined
+by SDK initialization on playback. No driver DLL is bundled or injected, no
+driver settings are changed, and the SDK's independent FPS cap stays disabled.
+File playback and still images do not invoke this input delay. The default is
+off. The two new preferences are separate from enhancement presets and retain
+old preference values and NVIDIA behavior.
+
+## Validation performed here
+
+- `python scripts/run-latency-contracts.py --output <private task directory>`:
+  24 AntiLagSession checks and 325 XeSS fallback cadence checks, all passed.
+- GCC C++20 builds use `-Wall -Wextra -Werror -pedantic`.
+- Lifecycle coverage includes unsupported initialization, waiting input,
+  3 generated + 1 real presents, cancellation, update/marker failures, release,
+  repeated enable/close and sustained stalls.
+- Timing coverage includes exact 30 → 120 slots, late readiness, pause/resume,
+  multiplier changes, invalid inputs and a ten-second source-grid simulation.
+- `python -B scripts/i18n/extract.py --check`: no missing English, Traditional
+  Chinese or Japanese translations; no placeholder errors. Four already-unused
+  startup-music entries from the release catalog are retained unchanged.
+- `git diff --check`: passed.
+
+New Qt Quick ownership/control tests are included, but were **not run**. Neither
+the native Windows driver adapter nor the modified player has been compiled in
+this environment. No Radeon, capture-card, display-scanout, physical latency,
+or visual smoothness results are claimed. CPU tests use a mocked driver API;
+they do not exercise AMD's driver. All portable binaries remain unmodified.
+
+## Windows build and validation handoff
+
+Apply the patch to the exact tag above, then use a separate Windows x64 build
+directory with MSVC 2022, C++20, Qt 6.8.3, the project's pinned XeSS SDK and the
+rest of its original SDK/dependency configuration. The portable ZIP contains
+runtime DLLs, not a complete compiler/SDK development environment. Preserve the
+patched FFmpeg dependencies and all original release features. See
+`docs/BUILD_2.1.9.md`, `docs/BUILD_2.1.9_2026-11-15.md` and `CMakeLists.txt` for the
+upstream dependency configuration; local E: paths in historical documents refer
+to the upstream developer's machine.
+
+From a configured Windows build:
+
+```powershell
+cmake --build <build-dir> --config Release --parallel 2 --target veyra_qml_ui veyra_anti_lag_tests veyra_xess_fallback_tests veyra_qml_quick_tests
+python scripts/run-latency-contracts.py --output <private-test-directory>
+```
+
+Use the upstream staging process to deploy the matching EXE, QML and embedded
+translation catalog together. Do not copy the new QML onto an original 2.1.9
+EXE: the original bridge has no corresponding new properties. Do not distribute
+or label a minimal build as the complete AMD portable release.
+
+On the user's Radeon hardware, compare baseline and candidate at 30 FPS input,
+XeSS 4X and 120 Hz output, using the same source, effects, resolution, VSync/VRR
+and driver settings. Turn the fallback preference off/on in the same candidate
+build; log `responsiveFallback`, `scheduled`, `bypassed` and `fallbackFrames`.
+If the fallback counter stays zero, this change cannot explain a difference.
+Measure source freshness and real displayed cadence, not merely four provider
+submissions. Check window/fullscreen, pause/resume, source gap, settings
+persistence, resize, seek and stop. Test native Anti-Lag separately with a
+Veyra-owned presentation backend; leave it out of XeSS latency comparisons.
+
+## Sources
+
+- https://github.com/Likely7/Veyra-NRVideo/releases/tag/v2.1.9
+- https://github.com/GPUOpen-LibrariesAndSDKs/AntiLag2-SDK
+- https://github.com/intel/xess/blob/main/doc/xell_developer_guide_english.md
+- https://github.com/intel/xess/blob/main/doc/xess_fg_developer_guide_english.md
